# Browser cache policy for this site's own static files - separate from
# candle-proxy.conf.example, which caches API responses on the SERVER
# side. This controls how long BROWSERS cache the app's own code.
#
# This assumes you're deploying the output of `npm run build` (dist/),
# not the raw source tree. That build step (scripts/build.js) renames
# every CSS/JS file to include a content hash (e.g. main.a1b2c3d4e5.js)
# and rewrites every reference to match - see the README's "Deploying"
# section. That makes the two policies below correct, not just fast:
#
#   - Every hashed file (css/*.js., js/**/*.js) can be cached
#     aggressively and treated as immutable, because a changed file is
#     *by construction* a different URL. There is nothing to invalidate.
#   - index.html itself is NOT hashed (it has to keep a stable URL to be
#     the entry point) and is the one file whose content changes without
#     its filename changing, so it stays no-cache: the browser
#     revalidates with the server on every load (a cheap 304 if
#     unchanged), which is how a new deploy's hashed references actually
#     reach the browser at all.
#
# (This replaces an earlier version of this file that used a single
# no-cache policy for all JS/CSS, back when filenames weren't hashed and
# there was no way to tell "changed" from "unchanged" except asking the
# server every time. If you're still deploying raw source rather than
# dist/, that older approach - no-cache on every .html/.css/.js - is the
# correct fallback; content-hashed immutable caching only works once the
# build step is actually part of your deploy.)
#
# Add inside the server {} block that serves this site (same one
# candle-proxy.conf.example's locations go in), with `root` pointing at
# the deployed dist/ directory:

location = /index.html {
    add_header Cache-Control "no-cache";
}

location ~* \.(?:css|js)$ {
    add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable";
}

# After editing, test and reload:
#   nginx -t && nginx -s reload
